registerChapter({
  id: 'beyond',
  num: '38',
  phase: 'Phase 9 · Beyond',
  title: 'Allocation, generalisation, and what is undone',
  blurb: 'Three things: a command that makes the frame rule false, the proof that the logic never needed heaps, and the one-line change to the language that unblocks the two capstones.',

  /* `K : PCM M` is a bound variable, so `K.op`, `K.unit` and the four law fields
     are projections rather than citations of any course lemma; the ledger checker
     cannot tell the difference and Unit 10's handover predicted these six. The
     seventh, `AExec.load`, is a constructor of an inductive type declared in an
     `illustration` block on this page. Nothing else is suppressed: no course
     lemma, no tactic and no shape row shares any of these names. */
  ledgerAllow: ['K.op', 'K.valid', 'K.unit', 'K.op_comm', 'K.op_assoc', 'K.unit_left', 'AExec.load'],

  orient: {
    youWill: [
      'Refute the framed triple for an allocator that names the location it takes, with a satisfiable precondition, so the failure is a false theorem rather than a vacuous one.',
      'Prove that <i>no</i> command of this language can satisfy that specification and be local, and read off which of <code>hoare_frame</code>&rsquo;s three hypotheses is the one that fails.',
      'State the three repairs — a side condition, a nondeterministic fresh location under an existential, finite heaps — and price each in theorems you would lose.',
      'Prove commutativity and the unit law of the separating conjunction over an <i>arbitrary</i> partial commutative monoid, and see the two heap lemmas turn into two structure fields.',
      'Say which laws of Modules 2 and 3 do <b>not</b> follow from the PCM axioms, with a compiled counterexample for associativity and one for cancellation.',
      'Give the one-line change to <code>Cmd</code> that lets a program follow a pointer, and separate the six theorems it obliges you to redo from the ones — every theorem stated over an arbitrary command — that it does not touch.'
    ],
    needs: [
      'Unit 27: <code>hoare_frame</code>, and its three hypotheses.',
      'Unit 24: <code>HeapLocal</code>, <code>Preserves</code>, <code>HeapOnly</code>, and why a syntactic side condition cannot be stated at <code>Store → Heap → Prop</code>.',
      'Unit 10: the <code>PCM</code> structure, its five laws, <code>heapPCM</code>, and <code>union_cancel_left</code>.',
      'Unit 14: the six slots of <code>∗</code>; Unit 15: <code>star_comm</code> and <code>star_emp_left</code>.',
      'Unit 20: <code>exec_deterministic</code>; Unit 35: <code>partial_of_total</code>.'
    ],
    payoff: 'You leave able to say, of any construct in this course, whether it was a fact about heaps or a fact about resources — and able to build a new resource model and get the whole logic for it.'
  },

  blocks: [

    /* ============================================================ opening ==== */

    {t:'p', h:"Two questions, then. The first is which of it was about heaps, and it has a sharp answer: the separating conjunction, its commutativity and its unit law need nothing from memory at all, and this page proves that by defining <code>∗</code> over an arbitrary partial commutative monoid and re-running the proofs. The second is what stops a program in this language walking a linked list, and it turns out to be one line of syntax."},

    {t:'p', h:"Between them sits something the course has asserted since Unit 24 and never proved: that locality is a property a language can <i>lose</i>. Unit 24 made the case with <code>x := allocated?(l)</code>, a command nobody wants, and argued in words. The command below is one every real language has, the demonstration is four lines of Lean, and what it breaks is the frame rule itself rather than a clause of a definition. That is where this page starts, because it is the only place where the central theorem of the course can be seen to break."},

    /* ============================================== allocation ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The command that breaks the frame rule'},

    {t:'p', h:"Nothing in this language makes a cell. <code>write</code> needs one already there, <code>load</code> needs one already there, <code>free</code> destroys one; heaps only ever shrink. So the first extension anybody would write is allocation, and the first specification anybody would write for it is the one that says which cell you got."},

    {t:'p', h:"Call the command <code>alloc-at l</code>: it takes the cell at address <code>l</code>, initialises it to zero, and is stuck if <code>l</code> is already allocated. Its small-footprint triple is as small as a triple can be — it starts owning nothing and ends owning one cell."},

    {t:'txt', cap:'The specification, and the same specification after one application of the frame rule with <code>R</code> chosen to be <code>l ↦ 7</code>. Every ingredient of the bottom line is legitimate: the top line is a total triple, <code>l ↦ 7</code> is an assertion about the heap alone, and the rule adds <code>∗ R</code> to both ends.',
     src:"                  { emp }   alloc-at l   { l ↦ 0 }\n     ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────  frame with  l ↦ 7\n     { emp ∗ l ↦ 7 }   alloc-at l   { (l ↦ 0) ∗ (l ↦ 7) }"},

    {t:'p', h:"Read the bottom line as the claim it is. The precondition holds of a real state: store everywhere zero, heap the single cell <code>l</code> holding 7. The postcondition holds of no state whatever, because <code>∗</code> cuts the heap into <i>disjoint</i> halves and both halves are asked for the address <code>l</code>. A total triple with a satisfiable precondition and an unsatisfiable postcondition is false, and the argument mentions no command anywhere — so it is not a fact about what <code>alloc-at</code> does, but about what the two assertions say."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The refutation, with the command replaced by an arbitrary relation <code>E</code> on states. The three lines after <code>intro</code> are: feed the precondition its witness, take the final state the triple promises, and hand its store and heap to Unit 14&rsquo;s <code>star_same_loc_absurd</code>. No <code>Cmd</code>, no <code>Exec</code>, no semantics for allocation is needed, because none of them could help.',
     src:"theorem no_semantics_survives_framing (l : Loc) (E : State → State → Prop) :\n    ¬ (∀ σ h, (emp ∗ (l ↦ 7)) σ h →\n        ∃ s', E ⟨σ, h⟩ s' ∧ ((l ↦ 0) ∗ (l ↦ 7)) s'.store s'.heap) := by\n  intro hall\n  obtain ⟨s', _, hq⟩ := hall (fun _ => 0) (Heap.singleton l 7)\n    (star_emp_left_intro (l ↦ 7) (fun _ => 0) (Heap.singleton l 7) rfl)\n  exact star_same_loc_absurd l 0 7 s'.store s'.heap hq"},

    {t:'p', h:"Both halves of &ldquo;satisfiable precondition, unsatisfiable postcondition&rdquo; are theorems you already have. <code>star_emp_left_intro</code> supplies the witness — it is the introduction rule for <code>emp ∗ P</code>, proved at Unit 15 — and <code>star_same_loc_absurd</code> supplies the contradiction, proved at Unit 14 as the theorem that says separation implies non-aliasing. The refutation is those two lemmas facing each other."},

    {t:'p', h:"So the framed triple is false. But the frame rule is <i>proved</i>, and its proof does not inspect the command. Which of its three hypotheses is the one that cannot hold?"},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The frame rule, applied to a hypothetical local allocator, produces a contradiction. <code>Preserves c (l ↦ 7)</code> is discharged on the spot: <code>l ↦ 7</code> is an assertion about the heap alone, so Unit 24&rsquo;s <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> supplies it for every command. The unframed triple <code>hc</code> is a hypothesis. What is left is <code>HeapLocal c</code>.',
     src:"theorem no_local_allocator (l : Loc) (c : Cmd)\n    (hc : Hoare emp c (l ↦ 0)) (hlocal : HeapLocal c) : False :=\n  no_semantics_survives_framing l (Exec c)\n    (hoare_frame hc hlocal (preserves_of_heapOnly c (heapOnly_pointsTo l 7)))"},

    {t:'p', h:"That is the whole diagnosis, and it is a theorem rather than a story: <b>no command satisfying <code>{emp} c {l ↦ 0}</code> is local.</b> The statement is about this language, where it is vacuously safe — no such command exists in <code>Cmd</code>, which is why the corpus is consistent — but the proof reads only <code>hoare_frame</code>'s statement, and <code>hoare_frame</code>'s proof reads only its three hypotheses. Extend <code>Cmd</code> with <code>alloc-at</code> and the same four lines still compile, and <code>hc</code> becomes provable. The theorem then stops being vacuous and turns into what it was always going to be: a proof that <code>alloc-at</code> is not local."},

    {t:'p', h:"Why does locality fail? The clause that does the work is the one about the frame: the run under a frame must do what the run without it did. Which addresses are free depends on the whole heap, so adding a frame changes which addresses are free, and a command that must take <i>this</i> address is stuck exactly where the frame was in the way. Any specification that names the address it allocates is therefore a specification no local command can meet — not because the implementation is bad, but because the specification is a claim about a global fact."},

    /* -------------------------------------------------- the repairs ---- */

    {t:'h3', s:'Three repairs, and what each costs'},

    {t:'p', h:"No local command meets a specification that names the address it allocates. So either the frame rule is restricted, or the specification stops naming the address — and once it stops naming it, the model has to guarantee that there is an address to take. Three changes, and each has been made by some real development."},

    {t:'dl', items:[
      {k:'Restrict the rule with a side condition', h:"Keep <code>alloc-at l</code> and its specification, and forbid framing with any <code>R</code> that mentions <code>l</code>. This is sound and it is what a paper proof would write. The cost is the thing separation logic was built to buy: the side condition is a claim about which addresses appear in <code>R</code>, so every use of the rule needs to know what the frame is made of, and the frame stops being an opaque <i>everything else</i>. Unit 24 also showed that the condition cannot even be <i>stated</i> here — an <code>Assertion</code> is a function <code>Store → Heap → Prop</code>, and a function has no syntax to inspect."},
      {k:'Make allocation nondeterministic and the postcondition existential', h:"Let the command take any free address and let the specification say only that it took one: <code>{emp} alloc x {∃ l, x = l ∗ l ↦ 0}</code>, in the notation Unit 23 writes its rules in. Locality is restored, because the framed run is allowed to pick a different address from the unframed one, and the existential does not care which. The cost is <code>exec_deterministic</code>, and with it everything proved from it — <code>partial_of_total</code> most immediately, which is the bridge between the two readings of a triple, and which needs the final state to be unique."},
      {k:'Make heaps finite', h:"Under <code>Heap := Loc → Option Val</code> there is a heap in which every address is taken, so &ldquo;a free address exists&rdquo; is false in this model and a <b>total</b> triple for allocation cannot be proved even with the existential. Finiteness makes it true. The cost is <code>funext</code>: a finite heap is a structure carrying a bound and a proof, so two heaps are no longer equal because they agree at every address, and every equation in Modules 1 and 2 changes shape."}
    ]},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Why the third repair is not optional. The everywhere-allocated heap is one line, and it has no free address, so in this model there are states in which allocation cannot run at all.',
     src:"def fullHeap : Heap := fun _ => some 0\n\ntheorem fullHeap_has_no_free_location : ¬ ∃ l, fullHeap l = none := by\n  intro ⟨l, hl⟩\n  exact absurd hl (by simp [fullHeap])"},

    {t:'p', h:"The existential in the second repair is load-bearing. It is not there because the implementation happens not to promise an address. It is there because <i>framing changes the answer</i>: the same command, run under a bigger heap, must be allowed to take a different cell, and a postcondition that fixes the cell forbids exactly that. Under the existential, the framed postcondition becomes satisfiable again, and the witness that satisfies it is a location the frame does not own."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The framed postcondition with the address existentially quantified. Compare the earlier refutation: the same frame <code>l ↦ 7</code>, and now a state satisfying the conjunction, with the allocated cell at <code>l + 1</code>. The two heaps are disjoint because the addresses differ, which is <code>singleton_disjoint</code> from Unit 08.',
     src:"theorem framed_fresh_postcondition_holds (l : Loc) :\n    ((aExists fun k => (k ↦ 0)) ∗ (l ↦ 7)) (fun _ => 0)\n      (Heap.union (Heap.singleton (l + 1) 0) (Heap.singleton l 7)) :=\n  ⟨Heap.singleton (l + 1) 0, Heap.singleton l 7,\n   singleton_disjoint 0 7 (by simp), rfl, ⟨l + 1, rfl⟩, rfl⟩"},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:'What the second repair looks like in Lean, and why it is not in the corpus. Adding a constructor to <code>Cmd</code> and a rule to <code>Exec</code> invalidates every proof that takes a run apart: every <code>cases hex</code> from Unit 19 onwards gains a branch, and there are twenty-three of those alone. Fed to Lean as it stands it gives four errors, and they are the honest ones: <code>Cmd</code> has already been declared, <code>Exec</code> has already been declared, <code>unexpected end of input; expected \':=\', \'where\' or \'|\'</code> under the unproved theorem, and <code>Unknown constant Cmd.alloc</code> because the redeclaration failed.',
     src:"inductive Cmd where\n  | alloc : Var → Cmd\n  -- … and the eight constructors of Unit 18\n\ninductive Exec : Cmd → State → State → Prop where\n  | alloc {s x l} (hfree : s.heap l = none) :\n      Exec (.alloc x) s ⟨Store.set s.store x l, Heap.write s.heap l 0⟩\n  -- … and the ten rules of Unit 19\n\ntheorem hoare_alloc (x : Var) :\n    Hoare (emp) (.alloc x)\n      (aExists fun l => pure (fun σ => σ x = l) ∗ (l ↦ 0))"},

    {t:'p', h:"The premise <code>hfree</code> is where the nondeterminism lives: it constrains <code>l</code> to be free and says nothing else, so one state has many successors and <code>exec_deterministic</code> is refuted by the first two of them."},

    {t:'detail', title:'The finite-heap structure, and what it does to every proof in Modules 1 and 2', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:'One way to make heaps finite: carry a bound and a proof that nothing beyond it is allocated. Fed to Lean against this course it gives one error, <code>Heap has already been declared</code> — the name has been an <code>abbrev</code> for a function type since Unit 00, and this is a different type with the same name rather than a refinement of it.',
       src:"structure Heap where\n  lookup : Loc → Option Val\n  bound  : Loc\n  free_beyond : ∀ l, bound ≤ l → lookup l = none"},
      {t:'p', h:"Two heaps are now equal when their <code>lookup</code> fields agree <i>and</i> their <code>bound</code> fields agree, and the second is not something you want to prove — the same partial map has many legal bounds. The usual repair is to make the bound existential, a single field of type <code>∃ n, ∀ l, n ≤ l → lookup l = none</code>. That field lives in <code>Prop</code>, and Lean treats any two proofs of the same proposition as the same term, so it cannot make two heaps differ: equality is back to equality of <code>lookup</code>. What changes is the first line of every proof. <code>funext l</code> compares two functions; two structures are compared by comparing their fields, so each proof in Modules 1 and 2 that opened with <code>funext l</code> now opens by reducing the structures to their <code>lookup</code> fields and then does what it did before. The mathematics does not change. Roughly two hundred lines of it are rewritten."},
      {t:'p', h:"There is a cheaper alternative that some developments take: keep infinite heaps and weaken allocation to a <b>partial</b> triple, which says only that if the command runs it lands correctly. That costs nothing structurally and buys nothing either — the whole point of a total triple for allocation is that allocation succeeds."}
    ]},

    /* ============================================== the generalisation ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Which of it was about heaps'},

    {t:'p', h:"Unit 10 ended by packaging the heap laws as a structure, instantiating it once, and then never using it. That was deliberate: the point of naming a structure is that other things satisfy it, and there was nothing yet to say about the others. There is now. Module 3's laws were proved from lemmas about <code>Heap.union</code> and <code>Heap.disjoint</code>, and Unit 10 collected <i>some</i> of those lemmas into the structure. Which of the laws survive when the structure is all you have is therefore a real question, and the answer is not all of them."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:'Three data fields and five laws, from Unit 10. <code>op</code> is total and <code>valid</code> says which pairs are meant to be combined — the split that Units 08 and 09 forced on <code>Heap.disjoint</code> and <code>Heap.union</code>, made into an interface.',
     src:"structure PCM (M : Type) where\n  op         : M → M → M\n  unit       : M\n  valid      : M → M → Prop\n  op_comm    : ∀ a b, valid a b → op a b = op b a\n  op_assoc   : ∀ a b c, op (op a b) c = op a (op b c)\n  unit_left  : ∀ a, op unit a = a\n  valid_unit : ∀ a, valid unit a\n  valid_comm : ∀ a b, valid a b → valid b a"},

    {t:'p', h:"The separating conjunction of two predicates over a PCM is the same sentence as Unit 14's, with three names changed. <i>There exist two pieces, they may be combined, the resource in hand is their combination, and the two predicates hold of the two pieces.</i> Every occurrence of <code>Heap</code> becomes <code>M</code>, <code>Heap.disjoint</code> becomes <code>K.valid</code>, <code>Heap.union</code> becomes <code>K.op</code> — and the store binder disappears, because a store is not a resource and never was."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:'The definition. <code>K</code> is the resource algebra and travels as an explicit argument; <code>P</code> and <code>Q</code> are predicates on resources rather than on states.',
     src:"def pcmStar {M : Type} (K : PCM M) (P Q : M → Prop) : M → Prop :=\n  fun m => ∃ m₁ m₂, K.valid m₁ m₂ ∧ m = K.op m₁ m₂ ∧ P m₁ ∧ Q m₂"},

    {t:'p', h:"How close is that to <code>star</code>? Not similar: identical. Fix a store, instantiate the algebra at <code>heapPCM</code>, and the two definitions are the same term — which Lean will confirm without being given a proof, because <code>heapPCM</code>'s fields reduce to the heap operations by definition."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The general definition, instantiated at heaps, <i>is</i> Unit 14&rsquo;s. <code>rfl</code> is the whole proof: no unfolding lemma, no <code>simp</code>, no <code>⊣⊢</code>. Everything Module 3 proves about <code>∗</code> is a statement about this term.',
     src:"example (P Q : Assertion) (σ : Store) : pcmStar heapPCM (P σ) (Q σ) = (P ∗ Q) σ := rfl"},

    {t:'p', h:"Now the laws. The one worth doing by hand is commutativity, because it is the law that consumes a hypothesis: <code>K.op_comm</code> is a conditional equation, and the condition is validity."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x74',
     name:'pcmStar_comm',
     hard:false,
     why:"This is the generalisation claim, as a theorem rather than a remark. When it closes you have shown that the commutativity of <code>∗</code> — the first law of Module 3, and the one that took a <code>disjoint_symm</code> and a <code>union_comm</code> — needs nothing about memory beyond two fields of a structure. Everything that follows on this page, including the ghost-state and concurrency claim, rests on this proof and its companion in <code>x75</code>.",
     setup:"<code>K : PCM M</code> is a bound variable, so the laws arrive as field projections: <code>K.op_comm</code>, <code>K.valid_comm</code>. There are no heap lemmas in scope for an arbitrary <code>M</code> and no <code>simp</code> set that knows anything about it. Six slots in, six slots out.",
     goal:"theorem pcmStar_comm {M : Type} (K : PCM M) (P Q : M → Prop) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K P Q m → pcmStar K Q P m := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, the hypothesis is <code>∃ m₁ m₂, K.valid m₁ m₂ ∧ m = K.op m₁ m₂ ∧ P m₁ ∧ Q m₂</code> and the goal is the same thing with <code>P</code> and <code>Q</code> exchanged: <code>∃ m₁ m₂, K.valid m₁ m₂ ∧ m = K.op m₁ m₂ ∧ Q m₁ ∧ P m₂</code>. Nothing is hidden; both sides are one existential with four conjuncts.",
       "Hand the two pieces back in the other order. Three of the four conjuncts then need work: the swapped pair must be shown valid, and <code>m</code> must be shown equal to the composite <i>in the new order</i>. The two facts about the pieces travel unchanged, into the opposite slots.",
       "Take the hypothesis apart with a six-name <code>intro</code> pattern and build the answer as one anonymous constructor. The two obligations are <code>K.valid_comm</code> and <code>K.op_comm</code>; both take the two elements and then the validity proof, and <code>K.op_comm</code> needs it because the equation it states is conditional.",
       "<code>intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, hp, hq⟩</code>. That leaves <code>⊢ pcmStar K Q P m</code> with <code>hv : K.valid m₁ m₂</code>, <code>hm : m = K.op m₁ m₂</code>, <code>hp : P m₁</code> and <code>hq : Q m₂</code> in context. The answer is <code>exact ⟨m₂, m₁, …, …, hq, hp⟩</code> with two holes, and the second hole is an equation you close with <code>rw</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem pcmStar_comm {M : Type} (K : PCM M) (P Q : M → Prop) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K P Q m → pcmStar K Q P m := by\n  intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, hp, hq⟩\n  exact ⟨m₂, m₁, K.valid_comm _ _ hv, by rw [hm, K.op_comm _ _ hv], hq, hp⟩",
     solNote:"Two lines, and both underscores in <code>K.op_comm _ _ hv</code> are forced: the field takes the two elements explicitly and Lean recovers them from <code>hv</code>'s type.",
     expl:"Two structure fields do the work two heap lemmas did, and the shape of the proof is untouched: take the split apart, hand the pieces back in the other order, repair the equation that moved with them. Nothing in the proof can see what <code>M</code> is, which is the point.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, hp, hq⟩', h:"One tactic doing two jobs. <code>intro</code> takes the implication's antecedent, and the pattern destructures it on the way in — two existential witnesses and then four conjuncts, flattened, so six names for what is nominally a nested pair. The goal left is <code>pcmStar K Q P m</code>, still folded."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨m₂, m₁, K.valid_comm _ _ hv, by rw [hm, K.op_comm _ _ hv], hq, hp⟩', h:"The mirror image of the pattern. Slots one and two are the pieces, swapped. Slot three turns <code>K.valid m₁ m₂</code> into <code>K.valid m₂ m₁</code>. Slot four is an equation and gets its own <code>by</code>: <code>rw [hm]</code> replaces <code>m</code> on the left by <code>K.op m₁ m₂</code>, and <code>rw [K.op_comm _ _ hv]</code> rewrites that to <code>K.op m₂ m₁</code>, at which point <code>rw</code>'s silent trailing <code>rfl</code> closes it. Slots five and six are <code>hq</code> and <code>hp</code>, in the opposite order from the one they arrived in."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'pcmStar_comm, with the equation slot opened as a hole',
        start:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP Q : M → Prop\nm : M\n⊢ pcmStar K P Q m → pcmStar K Q P m",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, hp, hq⟩',
           state:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP Q : M → Prop\nm m₁ m₂ : M\nhv : K.valid m₁ m₂\nhm : m = K.op m₁ m₂\nhp : P m₁\nhq : Q m₂\n⊢ pcmStar K Q P m",
           h:"Six new hypotheses and a goal that has not moved. The goal is still folded behind <code>pcmStar</code>, and nothing needs to unfold it: the anonymous constructor will."},
          {tac:'refine ⟨m₂, m₁, K.valid_comm _ _ hv, ?_, hq, hp⟩',
           state:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP Q : M → Prop\nm m₁ m₂ : M\nhv : K.valid m₁ m₂\nhm : m = K.op m₁ m₂\nhp : P m₁\nhq : Q m₂\n⊢ m = K.op m₂ m₁",
           h:"Five of the six slots are filled by terms and the sixth is left open, which is what makes the equation visible. This is the solution's <code>exact</code> with its <code>by</code> block turned into a hole; the two proofs are the same proof."},
          {tac:'rw [hm, K.op_comm _ _ hv]',
           state:"No goals.",
           h:"<code>rw [hm]</code> rewrites left to right, so the <code>m</code> on the left becomes <code>K.op m₁ m₂</code> and the goal reads <code>K.op m₁ m₂ = K.op m₂ m₁</code>. The second rewrite turns the left side into <code>K.op m₂ m₁</code>, and <code>rw</code> finishes with <code>rfl</code> on its own."}
        ],
        done:"No goals."},
       {t:'p', h:"Dropping the validity argument is instructive rather than fatal. <code>rw [hm, K.op_comm]</code> still rewrites — <code>rw</code> is happy to use a conditional equation and hands you the condition as a new goal:"},
       {t:'state', cap:'Compiling the solution with <code>K.op_comm</code> in place of <code>K.op_comm _ _ hv</code>. The line-and-column prefix is dropped here and everywhere below.',
        src:"error: unsolved goals\ncase a\nM : Type\nK : PCM M\nP Q : M → Prop\nm m₁ m₂ : M\nhv : K.valid m₁ m₂\nhm : m = K.op m₁ m₂\nhp : P m₁\nhq : Q m₂\n⊢ K.valid m₁ m₂"},
       {t:'p', h:"The leftover goal is <code>hv</code>, exactly. <code>by rw [hm, K.op_comm]; exact hv</code> is therefore a second correct answer, and seeing the premise appear as <code>case a</code> is worth more than avoiding it: it is what a conditional rewrite looks like when you do not discharge it in place."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Swapping the pieces and forgetting that the equation moves with them. Writing <code>⟨m₂, m₁, K.valid_comm _ _ hv, hm, hq, hp⟩</code> reuses <code>hm</code> unchanged, and Lean reports it at the fourth slot: <code>Application type mismatch: The argument hm has type m = K.op m₁ m₂ but is expected to have type m = K.op m₂ m₁ in the application And.intro hm</code>. The message names <code>And.intro</code> rather than the tuple because the flattened <code>⟨…⟩</code> is nested pairs underneath, so the slot number in your source and the constructor in the error do not line up. · The other one is putting <code>hp</code> and <code>hq</code> back in the order they came out rather than swapped. That lands at the fifth slot with the same shape of message — <code>the argument hp has type P m₁ but is expected to have type Q m₂ in the application And.intro hp</code> — and it is worth reading the two errors together, because they are the two halves of one mistake: the pieces were exchanged and everything said about them has to follow. · A different miss, and the one that arrives first if it arrives at all: writing <code>K.valid_comm hv</code> with the underscores left out. The two elements are <i>explicit</i> arguments of the field, so <code>hv</code> is read as the first of them, and the complaint is about a category error rather than about separation logic: <code>Application type mismatch: The argument hv has type K.valid m₁ m₂ of sort `Prop` but is expected to have type M of sort `Type` in the application K.valid_comm hv</code>. Read <i>of sort <code>Prop</code> … of sort <code>Type</code></i> as <i>you have put a proof where an element goes</i>; <code>K.op_comm hv</code> fails identically.",
     variants:"Reverse the orientation of the rewrite — <code>by rw [hm, K.op_comm _ _ (K.valid_comm _ _ hv)]</code> — and it still closes, because that equation is <code>K.op m₂ m₁ = K.op m₁ m₂</code> and <code>rw</code> finds its left side on the goal's <i>right</i>. Both directions compile; there is no orientation to get right here, which is unusual. · The <i>order</i> of the two rewrites is not free in the same way, and that is the one thing in this proof that has to be arranged. <code>rw [K.op_comm _ _ hv, hm]</code> is the same two equations the other way round, and it fails on the first of them: at that moment the goal is still <code>m = K.op m₂ m₁</code>, which contains no <code>K.op m₁ m₂</code> anywhere, and Lean says <code>Tactic `rewrite` failed: Did not find an occurrence of the pattern K.op m₁ m₂ in the target expression m = K.op m₂ m₁</code>. <code>hm</code> has to go first because it is what puts a <code>K.op</code> on the left-hand side at all; <code>op_comm</code> has nothing to act on until it has. · Delete <code>valid_comm</code> from the structure and the theorem dies at slot three with nothing to replace it: validity of <code>m₁</code> with <code>m₂</code> would give you no information about <code>m₂</code> with <code>m₁</code>, and <code>op_comm</code> cannot be applied because its own premise is unavailable. Heaps make this look free, since <code>disjoint_symm</code> is one line, but freeness is a property of that model and not of the axioms. · Delete the validity conjunct from <code>pcmStar</code> itself and commutativity survives — you would be proving it for the connective Unit 14 called <code>starNoDisj</code>, which is commutative and useless.",
    },

    {t:'p', h:"Set the two proofs side by side. The bodies are two lines each and differ in two lemma citations and a binder, which is the sharpest available answer to <i>which of it was about heaps</i>."},

    {t:'cmp',
     left: {t:'Unit 15 · star_comm', kind:'good', h:"Two heap lemmas, and a store binder that is threaded through and never used.",
            src:"theorem star_comm (P Q : Assertion) : P ∗ Q ⊢ Q ∗ P := by\n  intro σ h ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp, hq⟩\n  exact ⟨h₂, h₁, disjoint_symm hd, by rw [hu, union_comm hd], hq, hp⟩"},
     right:{t:'Unit 38 · pcmStar_comm', h:"Two structure fields, and no store at all.",
            src:"theorem pcmStar_comm {M : Type} (K : PCM M) (P Q : M → Prop) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K P Q m → pcmStar K Q P m := by\n  intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, hp, hq⟩\n  exact ⟨m₂, m₁, K.valid_comm _ _ hv, by rw [hm, K.op_comm _ _ hv], hq, hp⟩"}},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>disjoint_symm</code> became <code>K.valid_comm</code>; <code>union_comm</code> became <code>K.op_comm</code>; <code>σ</code> and <code>h</code> became <code>m</code>. The entailment <code>⊢</code> unfolds to a universally quantified implication over stores and heaps, which is why the left-hand <code>intro</code> takes three things and the right-hand one takes one. Nothing else moved."},

    {t:'p', h:"The unit law is the other half of the claim, and it comes with a second task: build a resource algebra that has nothing to do with memory and watch both theorems apply to it unchanged."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x75',
     name:'pcmStar_unit_left, and a PCM of your own',
     hard:false,
     why:"Commutativity alone could be a coincidence of one law. Two laws, one of them the one that consumes the unit, is a pattern — and the second half of this exercise is what turns the pattern into something you can use: a <code>PCM</code> you built, with the whole of Module 3's algebra available for it for free. That is the licence for ghost state, for permissions, and for concurrent separation logic.",
     setup:"Two parts. <b>Part one</b> is the theorem below: the assertion &ldquo;this piece is the unit&rdquo; is written <code>fun x => x = K.unit</code>, since there is no <code>emp</code> for an arbitrary <code>M</code>. Prove it. <b>Part two</b> has no marked answer and the editor does not check it: define a second <code>PCM</code> of your own and confirm that <code>pcmStar_comm</code> and <code>pcmStar_unit_left</code> apply to it. The suggested one is <code>(Nat, +, 0)</code> with every pair valid — resources that count rather than resources that are owned. Any structure satisfying all five laws will do, and the <b>Why it works</b> panel shows two worked answers, not the answer — one in which every pair is valid and one in which validity is a real constraint, which is the difference between a resource you count and a resource you own.",
     goal:"theorem pcmStar_unit_left {M : Type} (K : PCM M) (P : M → Prop) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K (fun x => x = K.unit) P m → P m := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, the hypothesis says: <code>m</code> splits into <code>m₁</code> and <code>m₂</code>, they are valid together, <code>m₁</code> is the unit, and <code>P</code> holds of <code>m₂</code>. The goal is <code>P m</code>. So the entire content is that <code>m</code> and <code>m₂</code> are the same element. Part two asks for four pieces of data and five proofs: a type, a binary operation on it, a distinguished element, a relation saying which pairs may be combined, and one proof per law.",
       "Substitute the unit for <code>m₁</code>, then use the law that says composing with the unit on the left changes nothing. That turns <code>m = K.op m₁ m₂</code> into <code>m = m₂</code>, and the goal <code>P m</code> into <code>P m₂</code>, which you already have. For part two, the cheapest honest answer is a commutative monoid you already trust with the validity relation set to hold of <i>every</i> pair: nothing among the five laws forbids that, and it is exactly what makes the answer cheap — the one conditional law is handed a hypothesis it can throw away.",
       "A six-name <code>intro</code> pattern; then <code>subst</code> on the hypothesis that says the left piece is the unit; then <code>rw</code> with the splitting equation and <code>K.unit_left</code>; then <code>exact</code>. The validity hypothesis is never read — name it <code>_</code>. For part two over <code>(Nat, +, 0)</code>, the three operation laws are <code>Nat.add_comm</code>, <code>Nat.add_assoc</code> and <code>Nat.zero_add</code>, each field being one of them applied to the field's own arguments; if <code>valid</code> is <code>fun _ _ => True</code> then the two validity fields are proofs of <code>True</code>, and the anonymous constructor with nothing in it is one.",
       "<code>intro ⟨m₁, m₂, _, hm, he, hp⟩</code>, leaving <code>⊢ P m</code> with <code>hm : m = K.op m₁ m₂</code>, <code>he : (fun x => x = K.unit) m₁</code> and <code>hp : P m₂</code>. Then <code>subst he</code> replaces every <code>m₁</code> by <code>K.unit</code>, and after it <code>hm</code> reads <code>m = K.op K.unit m₂</code>. Part two opens <code>def myPCM : PCM Nat where</code>, and what follows is the eight field names of the structure, in the order it declares them, each with a <code>:=</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem pcmStar_unit_left {M : Type} (K : PCM M) (P : M → Prop) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K (fun x => x = K.unit) P m → P m := by\n  intro ⟨m₁, m₂, _, hm, he, hp⟩\n  subst he\n  rw [hm, K.unit_left]\n  exact hp",
     solNote:"Four lines, and one of them is a rewrite of the goal rather than of a hypothesis: <code>rw [hm]</code> turns <code>P m</code> into <code>P (K.op K.unit m₂)</code>, and <code>K.unit_left</code> collapses that to <code>P m₂</code>. If you climbed all four hints and are still stuck at 2am, open this without guilt — the shape is the same as Unit 15's <code>star_emp_left</code>, and recognising that is worth more than rediscovering it.",
     expl:"The unit law of <code>∗</code>, with <code>emp</code> replaced by the only thing an arbitrary PCM has that could play its part: the predicate that holds of the unit and nothing else. Unit 15's <code>star_emp_left</code> is the same proof with <code>union_empty_left</code> where this one has <code>K.unit_left</code>, and in three lines rather than four — the <code>subst</code> here is a choice, not a necessity, and <code>variants</code> gives the three-line route.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro ⟨m₁, m₂, _, hm, he, hp⟩', h:"Six names for the six slots, and the third is an underscore because validity is never consulted: the unit law is unconditional, unlike commutativity. The goal stays <code>P m</code>."},
       {tac:'subst he', h:"<code>he</code> has type <code>(fun x => x = K.unit) m₁</code> — an equation wearing a beta-redex. <code>subst</code> reduces it, sees <code>m₁ = K.unit</code>, and eliminates <code>m₁</code> from the context altogether. The visible effect is on <code>hm</code>, which now reads <code>m = K.op K.unit m₂</code>."},
       {tac:'rw [hm, K.unit_left]', h:"The rewrite is on the <i>goal</i>, not on a hypothesis. <code>rw [hm]</code> replaces <code>m</code> by <code>K.op K.unit m₂</code>, giving <code>⊢ P (K.op K.unit m₂)</code>; <code>K.unit_left</code> is <code>∀ a, op unit a = a</code>, so it collapses the argument to <code>m₂</code> and the goal becomes <code>⊢ P m₂</code>."},
       {tac:'exact hp', h:"Which is <code>hp</code>. No structure field was used except <code>unit_left</code>."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'pcmStar_unit_left, tactic by tactic',
        start:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP : M → Prop\nm : M\n⊢ pcmStar K (fun x => x = K.unit) P m → P m",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro ⟨m₁, m₂, _, hm, he, hp⟩',
           state:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP : M → Prop\nm m₁ m₂ : M\nleft✝ : K.valid m₁ m₂\nhm : m = K.op m₁ m₂\nhe : (fun x => x = K.unit) m₁\nhp : P m₂\n⊢ P m",
           h:"The underscore becomes <code>left✝</code>, inaccessible and unused. <code>he</code> is displayed unreduced — the predicate applied to <code>m₁</code> rather than the equation it beta-reduces to — which is the one surprise on this page."},
          {tac:'subst he',
           state:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP : M → Prop\nm m₂ : M\nhp : P m₂\nleft✝ : K.valid K.unit m₂\nhm : m = K.op K.unit m₂\n⊢ P m",
           h:"<code>m₁</code> is gone from the binder list and every occurrence of it is now <code>K.unit</code>. The context has been reordered: <code>subst</code> re-adds the hypotheses it rewrote, so <code>hp</code> — which never mentioned <code>m₁</code> — now precedes them."},
          {tac:'rw [hm, K.unit_left]',
           state:"M : Type\nK : PCM M\nP : M → Prop\nm m₂ : M\nhp : P m₂\nleft✝ : K.valid K.unit m₂\nhm : m = K.op K.unit m₂\n⊢ P m₂",
           h:"Two rewrites in the goal and nothing else changes. <code>hm</code> survives in the context, still mentioning the <code>m</code> that no longer appears in the goal."}
        ],
        done:"No goals."},
       {t:'p', h:"Part two, one worked answer. The counting monoid: resources are natural numbers, combining is addition, the unit is zero, and every pair may be combined. Nothing is ever exclusive, so <code>valid</code> is constantly true — which is legitimate, and is what distinguishes a <i>counting</i> resource from an <i>owning</i> one."},
       {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'A PCM with no memory in it. Three of the five laws are core arithmetic; the two validity laws are <code>⟨⟩</code>, the proof of <code>True</code>. Both of this unit&rsquo;s theorems then apply to it with no further work: the second example composes 3 with the unit and reads the pair back the other way round, and the third cashes <code>pcmStar_unit_left</code> at <code>Nat</code>.',
        src:"def countPCM : PCM Nat where\n  op         := fun a b => a + b\n  unit       := 0\n  valid      := fun _ _ => True\n  op_comm    := fun a b _ => Nat.add_comm a b\n  op_assoc   := fun a b c => Nat.add_assoc a b c\n  unit_left  := fun a => Nat.zero_add a\n  valid_unit := fun _ => ⟨⟩\n  valid_comm := fun _ _ _ => ⟨⟩\n\nexample : pcmStar countPCM (fun m => m = 2) (fun m => m = 3) 5 :=\n  ⟨2, 3, ⟨⟩, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩\n\nexample : pcmStar countPCM (fun m => m = countPCM.unit) (fun m => m = 3) 3 :=\n  pcmStar_comm countPCM _ _ 3 ⟨3, 0, ⟨⟩, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩\n\nexample (P : Nat → Prop) (m : Nat) (h : pcmStar countPCM (fun x => x = countPCM.unit) P m) :\n    P m :=\n  pcmStar_unit_left countPCM P m h"},
       {t:'p', h:"Read the second example again. It is <code>x74</code> applied to something that is not a heap, twenty-three units after the same statement was proved for heaps as <code>star_comm</code>. Nothing was re-proved to get it. That is the payoff of Unit 10's structure, collected."},
       {t:'p', h:"Part two, a second worked answer, because the first one is cheap in one specific way: <code>countPCM</code> declares every pair valid, so <code>op_comm</code> is handed a hypothesis it discards and <code>pcmStar</code> never refuses a split. Here is a PCM where validity bites. One ghost cell, either held or not: resources are <code>Option Val</code>, combining takes whichever side has a value, and two resources may be combined only when at least one of them is <code>none</code>."},
       {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Exclusive ownership of a single cell, as a PCM. <code>op</code> is left-biased, so it is <i>not</i> commutative on all of <code>Option Val</code> — which is why <code>op_comm</code> is the one law that takes a hypothesis, and here the hypothesis is doing work. The first example is <code>x74</code> swapping a real split; the second is <code>x75</code> at this algebra; the third is what makes it ghost state rather than arithmetic.',
        src:"def exclPCM : PCM (Option Val) where\n  op         := fun a b => match a with | some v => some v | none => b\n  unit       := none\n  valid      := fun a b => a = none ∨ b = none\n  op_comm    := by\n    intro a b h\n    rcases h with h | h\n    · subst h; cases b <;> rfl\n    · subst h; cases a <;> rfl\n  op_assoc   := by intro a b c; cases a <;> rfl\n  unit_left  := fun _ => rfl\n  valid_unit := fun _ => Or.inl rfl\n  valid_comm := by\n    intro a b h\n    rcases h with h | h\n    · exact Or.inr h\n    · exact Or.inl h\n\nexample : pcmStar exclPCM (fun x => x = some 3) (fun x => x = none) (some 3) :=\n  pcmStar_comm exclPCM _ _ (some 3) ⟨none, some 3, Or.inl rfl, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩\n\nexample (P : Option Val → Prop) (a : Option Val)\n    (h : pcmStar exclPCM (fun x => x = exclPCM.unit) P a) : P a :=\n  pcmStar_unit_left exclPCM P a h\n\ntheorem excl_not_duplicable (v : Val) (a : Option Val) :\n    ¬ pcmStar exclPCM (fun x => x = some v) (fun x => x = some v) a := by\n  intro ⟨_, _, hv, _, h1, h2⟩\n  subst h1; subst h2\n  rcases hv with h | h <;> exact absurd h (by simp)"},
       {t:'p', h:"<code>K.op (some 3) (some 4)</code> is <code>some 3</code> and the other order is <code>some 4</code>, so the operation is genuinely non-commutative; commutativity holds exactly on the pairs <code>valid</code> admits, and the swap in the first example is legal only because of the <code>Or.inl rfl</code> handed to it. The last theorem is the contrast worth keeping. Over <code>countPCM</code>, <code>⟨2, 2, ⟨⟩, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩</code> proves <code>pcmStar countPCM (fun m => m = 2) (fun m => m = 2) 4</code> — a counting resource splits into two copies of itself. Over <code>exclPCM</code> the same claim has no proof at <i>any</i> resource, and the component that refuses is the third one. Which is the whole of what ownership means, stated without mentioning memory."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Reaching for <code>exact hp</code> immediately. It has type <code>P m₂</code>, the goal is <code>P m</code>, and Lean says <code>Type mismatch: hp has type P m₂ but is expected to have type P m</code> — a message that names two things which are equal and does not say so, because they are equal only by <code>hm</code>, which nothing has applied yet. · <code>subst hm</code> instead of <code>subst he</code> also works, and leads somewhere different: it eliminates <code>m</code>, leaving <code>⊢ P (K.op m₁ m₂)</code> with <code>he</code> still standing, after which <code>rw [he, K.unit_left]</code> finishes. Compiled; neither route is wrong, and they are the same length. · In part two, <code>unit_left := fun _ => rfl</code> is refused with <code>Type mismatch: rfl has type ?m.20 = ?m.20 but is expected to have type 0 + x✝ = x✝</code>. <code>0 + a</code> does not reduce to <code>a</code>, because <code>Nat</code> addition recurses on its second argument, so <code>Nat.zero_add</code> is a theorem rather than a definitional fact. · Five names instead of six. <code>intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hm, he, hp⟩</code> is accepted without a murmur: the pattern is shorter than the thing it takes apart, so the last name absorbs whatever is left, and the three you did write slide one slot to the left. <code>hm</code> catches the validity proof, <code>he</code> catches the splitting equation, and <code>hp</code> ends up as the conjunction <code>(fun x => x = K.unit) m₁ ∧ P m₂</code>. Nothing complains until the third line, and then it complains about the wrong hypothesis: <code>Invalid rewrite argument: Expected an equality or iff proof or definition name, but hm is a proof of K.valid m₁ m₂</code>. Count the conjuncts in the definition before you count the names — <code>pcmStar</code> has six slots and only the first two are visible as binders.",
     variants:"<code>rw [hm, he, K.unit_left]</code> with no <code>subst</code> at all also closes it — <code>rw</code> beta-reduces <code>he</code> for you and uses it as the equation <code>m₁ = K.unit</code> inside the goal. Compiled; either is fine. · The mirror image, <code>pcmStar K P (fun x => x = K.unit) m → P m</code>, is <b>not</b> a new proof: it is <code>pcmStar_unit_left</code> composed with <code>pcmStar_comm</code>, and right neutrality of a PCM is derived rather than assumed, exactly as Unit 10 showed. · Drop <code>valid_unit</code> from the structure, so that the unit is no longer guaranteed to combine with anything, and this theorem survives untouched: its third slot is an underscore and validity is never read. What dies is the <i>other</i> direction. <code>P m → pcmStar K (fun x => x = K.unit) P m</code> is <code>fun hp => ⟨K.unit, m, K.valid_unit m, (K.unit_left m).symm, rfl, hp⟩</code> — compiled, one line — and the third component of that tuple is exactly the field you removed. · A third route for part one rewrites the hypothesis rather than the goal: after <code>subst he</code>, <code>rw [K.unit_left] at hm</code> collapses <code>hm</code> to <code>m = m₂</code>, and <code>exact hm ▸ hp</code> transports <code>hp</code> across it. Compiled, and the same length again — this proof has three correct shapes and no preferred one. · Part two has more than one right answer, and the two in <b>Why it works</b> differ where it matters. <code>countPCM</code> buys you the cheapest possible check that the laws are satisfiable at all; <code>exclPCM</code> buys you the case where <code>valid</code> is a real constraint, so that <code>op_comm</code>'s hypothesis is consumed and <code>pcmStar</code> refuses to duplicate a resource. A third answer with a different flavour again is <code>(Nat, ×, 1)</code> with every pair valid, which is <code>countPCM</code> with a different monoid and shows that the connective cares about the axioms and not about addition.",
    },

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'One more law, and the cheapest of the three: monotonicity uses no field of <code>PCM</code> at all. The split is handed back exactly as it arrived — same pieces, same validity proof, same equation — and only the two predicates are replaced, so there is nothing for the algebra to fail at. Unit 15&rsquo;s <code>star_mono</code> is this proof with a store binder in front of it.',
     src:"theorem pcmStar_mono {M : Type} (K : PCM M) {P P' Q Q' : M → Prop}\n    (hp : ∀ m, P m → P' m) (hq : ∀ m, Q m → Q' m) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K P Q m → pcmStar K P' Q' m := by\n  intro ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, h1, h2⟩\n  exact ⟨m₁, m₂, hv, hm, hp m₁ h1, hq m₂ h2⟩"},

    /* -------------------------------------------- what does not transfer ---- */

    {t:'h3', s:'What does not transfer'},

    {t:'p', h:"Two laws is not all of Module 3, and the honest half of the generalisation claim is the part that fails. The obvious candidate is associativity, and it is not a consequence of the five laws — not because the proof is hard, but because it is false."},

    {t:'p', h:"Trace where the proof would go. To turn <code>P ∗ (Q ∗ R)</code> into <code>(P ∗ Q) ∗ R</code> you are handed <code>valid m₁ (op m₂ m₃)</code> and <code>valid m₂ m₃</code>, and you need <code>valid m₁ m₂</code> and <code>valid (op m₁ m₂) m₃</code>. That is not <code>op_assoc</code>, which is about <code>op</code>; it is a statement about how <code>valid</code> distributes over <code>op</code>, and no field of <code>PCM</code> says anything of the kind. Unit 10 proved it for heaps under the names <code>disjoint_union_left</code> and <code>disjoint_union_right</code>, and said at the time that those two are not axioms of the structure. This is the bill for that."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'A PCM in which <code>pcmStar</code> is not associative. Resources are numbers under addition; the single pair (2, 2) is declared invalid, which costs nothing — the three operation laws hold unconditionally, the unit is 0 and so is valid with everything, and forbidding a symmetric pair leaves validity symmetric. Then 5 splits as 2 with (2 and 1), but not as (2 and 2) with 1.',
     src:"def clashPCM : PCM Nat where\n  op         := fun a b => a + b\n  unit       := 0\n  valid      := fun a b => ¬ (a = 2 ∧ b = 2)\n  op_comm    := fun a b _ => Nat.add_comm a b\n  op_assoc   := fun a b c => Nat.add_assoc a b c\n  unit_left  := fun a => Nat.zero_add a\n  valid_unit := fun _ hc => absurd hc.1 (by simp)\n  valid_comm := fun _ _ h hc => h ⟨hc.2, hc.1⟩\n\ntheorem pcmStar_not_assoc :\n    ¬ (∀ (M : Type) (K : PCM M) (P Q R : M → Prop) (m : M),\n        pcmStar K P (pcmStar K Q R) m → pcmStar K (pcmStar K P Q) R m) := by\n  intro hall\n  have h := hall Nat clashPCM (fun a => a = 2) (fun a => a = 2) (fun a => a = 1) 5\n    ⟨2, 3, (fun hc => absurd hc.2 (by simp)), rfl, rfl,\n     2, 1, (fun hc => absurd hc.2 (by simp)), rfl, rfl, rfl⟩\n  obtain ⟨_, _, _, _, ⟨_, _, hv, _, h1, h2⟩, _⟩ := h\n  subst h1; subst h2\n  exact hv ⟨rfl, rfl⟩"},

    {t:'p', h:"The refutation is short because the right-hand side is unsatisfiable outright: <code>pcmStar K P Q</code> would need two pieces both equal to 2, and that is the one pair <code>clashPCM</code> forbids. Meanwhile the left-hand side holds, because the pieces are grouped so that 2 never meets 2 directly. Regrouping is exactly what associativity does, and here regrouping is what breaks."},

    {t:'p', h:"Supply the missing law as a hypothesis and the proof appears, unchanged from the shape Unit 16 used. Heaps satisfy it, and the witness is the pair of bridge lemmas."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Associativity, from the axioms plus one distribution law; and the proof that heaps have that law. The second declaration is four lines and cites nothing this course did not already have at Unit 10.',
     src:"theorem pcmStar_assoc_of_distrib {M : Type} (K : PCM M)\n    (hdist : ∀ a b c, K.valid a (K.op b c) → K.valid b c →\n        K.valid a b ∧ K.valid (K.op a b) c)\n    (P Q R : M → Prop) (m : M) :\n    pcmStar K P (pcmStar K Q R) m → pcmStar K (pcmStar K P Q) R m := by\n  intro ⟨m₁, m', hv, hm, hp, m₂, m₃, hv', hm', hq, hr⟩\n  subst hm'\n  obtain ⟨h1, h2⟩ := hdist m₁ m₂ m₃ hv hv'\n  exact ⟨K.op m₁ m₂, m₃, h2, by rw [hm, K.op_assoc], ⟨m₁, m₂, h1, rfl, hp, hq⟩, hr⟩\n\ntheorem heapPCM_distrib : ∀ a b c, heapPCM.valid a (heapPCM.op b c) → heapPCM.valid b c →\n    heapPCM.valid a b ∧ heapPCM.valid (heapPCM.op a b) c := by\n  intro a b c hd hbc\n  obtain ⟨h1, h2⟩ := disjoint_union_right.mp hd\n  exact ⟨h1, disjoint_union_left.mpr ⟨h2, hbc⟩⟩"},

    {t:'p', h:"The second thing heaps have that a PCM need not is cancellation. <code>union_cancel_left</code> says that if you know the whole and one piece, the other piece is determined; at the level of assertions that is what makes a split by an exact assertion unique."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'Two ways of cutting the same heap that agree on the left piece agree on the right piece too. The three <code>subst</code>s replace the two left pieces by the singleton they are both equal to and then eliminate the heap itself, at which point <code>union_cancel_left</code> applies and <code>▸</code> transports the second assertion across the equation it returns.',
     src:"theorem split_is_unique (l : Loc) (v : Val) (Q R : Assertion) (σ : Store) (h : Heap)\n    (hq : ((l ↦ v) ∗ Q) σ h) (hr : ((l ↦ v) ∗ R) σ h) :\n    ∃ h₂, Q σ h₂ ∧ R σ h₂ := by\n  obtain ⟨a₁, a₂, hda, hua, hpa, hqa⟩ := hq\n  obtain ⟨b₁, b₂, hdb, hub, hpb, hrb⟩ := hr\n  subst hpa\n  subst hpb\n  subst hua\n  exact ⟨a₂, hqa, (union_cancel_left hda hdb hub) ▸ hrb⟩"},

    {t:'detail', title:'The PCM in which that fails', tag:'aside', open:false, blocks:[
      {t:'p', h:"Unit 10's <code>agreePCM</code> is the counterexample and it is worth having in front of you rather than remembered. Resources are <code>Option Val</code>, combining takes the left one if there is one, and two resources may be combined when one is absent or they agree. It satisfies all five laws."},
      {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The agreement PCM, from Unit 10, and cancellation refuted for it in three lines. <code>some 3</code> combined with itself and <code>some 3</code> combined with the unit give the same answer, and the two right-hand pieces differ.',
       src:"def agreePCM : PCM (Option Val) where\n  op         := fun a b => match a with | some v => some v | none => b\n  unit       := none\n  valid      := fun a b => a = none ∨ b = none ∨ a = b\n  op_comm    := by\n    intro a b h\n    rcases h with h | h | h\n    · subst h; cases b <;> rfl\n    · subst h; cases a <;> rfl\n    · subst h; cases a <;> rfl\n  op_assoc   := by intro a b c; cases a <;> rfl\n  unit_left  := fun _ => rfl\n  valid_unit := fun _ => Or.inl rfl\n  valid_comm := by\n    intro a b h\n    rcases h with h | h | h\n    · exact Or.inr (Or.inl h)\n    · exact Or.inl h\n    · exact Or.inr (Or.inr h.symm)\n\ntheorem agree_split_is_not_unique :\n    ¬ (∀ a b c : Option Val, agreePCM.valid a b → agreePCM.valid a c →\n        agreePCM.op a b = agreePCM.op a c → b = c) := by\n  intro hall\n  have h := hall (some 3) (some 3) none (Or.inr (Or.inr rfl)) (Or.inr (Or.inl rfl)) rfl\n  exact absurd h (by simp)"},
      {t:'p', h:"What this costs a logic built on a non-cancellative algebra: <i>the frame</i> stops being a definite thing. You can still frame, and the frame rule still holds, but from &ldquo;I own this piece of that resource&rdquo; you can no longer speak of the remainder as though it were determined. Assertions in such logics are routinely required to be <b>precise</b> — to pin down the piece they describe uniquely — and that requirement is doing the work cancellation does here for free."}
    ]},

    {t:'tbl', cap:'The inventory. The middle column is what a construct needs from the resource; the right column is where it comes from in this course.',
     head:['Construct', 'Needs', 'Status over an arbitrary PCM'],
     rows:[
       ['<code>∗</code> itself', 'a partial binary operation', '<b>transfers</b> — <code>pcmStar</code>, and at <code>heapPCM</code> it is Unit 14&rsquo;s definition on the nose'],
       ['commutativity', '<code>op_comm</code>, <code>valid_comm</code>', '<b>transfers</b> — <code>x74</code>'],
       ['the unit laws', '<code>unit_left</code>, <code>valid_unit</code>', '<b>transfers</b> — <code>x75</code>, with the mirror derived'],
       ['monotonicity and congruence', 'nothing at all', '<b>transfers</b> — <code>pcmStar_mono</code>, which cites no field of the structure'],
       ['associativity', 'validity distributing over <code>op</code>', '<b>fails</b> — <code>pcmStar_not_assoc</code>; recovered by one extra law, which heaps satisfy'],
       ['a determined frame', 'cancellation', '<b>fails</b> — <code>agreePCM</code>; recovered by demanding precise assertions'],
       ['<code>emp</code>, <code>↦</code>, <code>lseg</code>, <code>listRep</code>', 'addresses, cells, lookup', '<b>heaps only</b> — these name a particular <code>M</code> and cannot be stated in general'],
       ['<code>-∗</code>', '<code>op</code> and <code>valid</code>', '<b>definable</b> — but <code>wand_curry</code> and <code>wand_uncurry</code> regroup, so they cite <code>union_assoc</code> and both bridges and land in the row above'],
       ['<code>Hoare</code>, <code>HeapLocal</code>, <code>wp</code>, the frame rule', 'commands and a semantics', '<b>out of scope</b> — these quantify over <code>Exec</code>. Changing <code>M</code> does not touch them; changing the semantics rewrites all of them']
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"Which is what <code>x74</code> and <code>x75</code> bought. Swap <code>M</code> for something other than a heap and the top four rows come with you. Take <code>M</code> to be a map from locations to <i>fractions</i> and validity to be &ldquo;the fractions sum to at most one&rdquo;, and <code>∗</code> becomes fractional permissions, where several threads may hold read access to one cell. Take <code>M</code> to be a set of tokens under disjoint union and it becomes ghost state: assertions about things the program does not store, invented by the proof to record what a thread has done. Take <code>M</code> to be the states of a protocol and it becomes a concurrent separation logic, in which the frame is another thread's resources rather than another part of the heap."},

    {t:'note', kind:'key', title:'What the two theorems licence, and what they do not', h:"<code>x74</code> and <code>x75</code> are the reason one framework serves all of those: the algebra of <code>∗</code> is a function of the resource algebra and nothing else, so a new resource model inherits it. What they do not licence is the rest. Associativity has to be checked for each new <code>M</code>, cancellation usually fails and is replaced by precision, and the <i>program logic</i> — <code>Hoare</code>, <code>Exec</code>, locality, the frame rule as a theorem — is about commands and does not generalise by changing <code>M</code>. Concurrency in particular needs a semantics with interleaving, which is a change to <code>Exec</code>, not to <code>PCM</code>."},

    /* ============================================== address expressions ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The address the language cannot compute'},

    {t:'p', h:"Unit 33 built <code>lseg</code> and proved that two adjacent segments join; Unit 37 said what still stops the traversal they were built for. Here is that obstruction, in one line of Unit 18: <code>load</code> takes a <code>Var</code> and a <code>Loc</code>. The address is a literal in the syntax. A traversal needs <code>cur := [cur + 1]</code>, where the address is computed from a variable, and no term of <code>Cmd</code> says that."},

    {t:'txt', cap:'The change. Three constructors take an <code>Atom</code> where they took a <code>Loc</code>; <code>Atom</code> and its evaluator have existed since Unit 18 and already do arithmetic on store variables. The first line is the one that unblocks the walk — the other two are what a program needs to write and release at a computed address, which is the difference between the two capstones below.',
     src:"  | load   : Var → Loc  → Cmd            | load   : Var → Atom → Cmd\n  | write  : Loc → Atom → Cmd     ⟶      | write  : Atom → Atom → Cmd\n  | free   : Loc → Cmd                   | free   : Atom → Cmd"},

    {t:'p', h:"The semantics changes by evaluating the address before looking it up, and the primitive rules gain one conjunct in the precondition: a <code>fact</code> saying the address expression evaluates to the location the precondition owns. It owns nothing itself, so it is conjoined with <code>aAnd</code> rather than <code>∗</code>. Nothing else in the rule moves."},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:'The address-expression load, built as a one-constructor language beside the real one so that it compiles. <code>ahoare_load</code> is Unit 23&rsquo;s <code>hoare_load</code> with the precondition strengthened by <code>fact (fun σ => a.eval σ = l)</code>, and the only new <i>content</i> in its proof is the rewrite that turns the computed address back into <code>l</code>; the extra lines around it are the added conjunct being taken apart and the load&rsquo;s premise being stated at the address the semantics will evaluate.',
     src:"inductive ACmd where\n  | load : Var → Atom → ACmd\n\ninductive AExec : ACmd → State → State → Prop where\n  | load {s x a v} (hl : s.heap (a.eval s.store) = some v) :\n      AExec (.load x a) s ⟨Store.set s.store x v, s.heap⟩\n\ndef AHoare (P : Assertion) (c : ACmd) (Q : Assertion) : Prop :=\n  ∀ σ h, P σ h → ∃ s', AExec c ⟨σ, h⟩ s' ∧ Q s'.store s'.heap\n\ntheorem ahoare_load (x : Var) (a : Atom) (l : Loc) (v : Val) :\n    AHoare (aAnd (fact (fun σ => a.eval σ = l)) (l ↦ v)) (.load x a)\n           (pure (fun σ => σ x = v) ∗ (l ↦ v)) := by\n  intro σ h ⟨ha, hp⟩\n  subst hp\n  have ha' : a.eval σ = l := ha\n  refine ⟨⟨Store.set σ x v, Heap.singleton l v⟩, ?_, ?_⟩\n  · show AExec (.load x a) ⟨σ, Heap.singleton l v⟩ ⟨Store.set σ x v, Heap.singleton l v⟩\n    exact AExec.load (by rw [ha']; exact singleton_same l v)\n  · refine ⟨Heap.empty, Heap.singleton l v, disjoint_empty_left _, (union_empty_left _).symm, ⟨?_, rfl⟩, rfl⟩\n    show Store.set σ x v x = v\n    simp [Store.set]"},

    {t:'p', h:"Split the downstream theorems in two and the reason everything survives becomes a property of how the development is layered rather than luck. The ones stated <i>per constructor</i> change: the three primitive rules of Unit 23, and <code>heapLocal_load</code>, <code>heapLocal_write</code> and <code>heapLocal_free</code> from Units 24 and 25. Each gains the same line — evaluate the address, then proceed as before — and that is the ~40 lines the project below estimates. Everything stated over an <i>arbitrary</i> command changes not at all. <code>HeapLocal</code> quantifies over the state and the frame and never looks inside the command; <code>heapLocal_seq</code> and its two companions take locality of the parts as hypotheses and never name a constructor; <code>hoare_frame</code> reads three hypotheses; the assertion algebra of Module 3 does not know that commands exist."},

    /* ============================================== projects ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Three projects'},

    {t:'p', h:"These are not exercises. An exercise in this course ships with a verified solution; these ship with a scope estimate and a list of what breaks, because nobody has done them here. They are in increasing order of difficulty and the third depends on the second."},

    {t:'note', kind:'info', title:'Project 1 · Allocation', h:"Add <code>alloc : Var → Cmd</code> with the nondeterministic fresh-location rule sketched above, and prove <code>hoare_alloc</code> with its existential postcondition. <b>Scope:</b> about 150 lines. <b>What must be revisited:</b> <code>exec_deterministic</code> becomes false and must be deleted, taking <code>partial_of_total</code> with it — decide first whether you want the total or the partial reading, because you can no longer have both for free. <code>run_complete</code> goes the same way, and for the same reason: <code>run</code> is a function and has to pick one address, and the new rule admits every free one. <code>HeapLocal (.alloc x)</code> is a new obligation and the interesting one: proving it is where you discover that the framed run must be allowed to choose a different address, which is precisely why the postcondition is existential. If you also want the total triple to be provable under an arbitrary frame, heaps must be made finite first, which is a second project of comparable size."},

    {t:'note', kind:'info', title:'Project 2 · Address expressions', h:"Change <code>load</code>, <code>write</code> and <code>free</code> to take an <code>Atom</code>, as above. <b>Scope:</b> about 40 lines of mechanical refactor, plus a <code>fact</code> conjunct on the precondition of each of the three primitive rules. <b>What must be revisited:</b> the three rules of Unit 23; <code>heapLocal_load</code> from Unit 24 and both proofs of Unit 25; the symbolic-execution examples of Unit 29, whose specifications gain a <code>fact</code> conjunct. <b>What must not:</b> <code>heapLocal_skip</code> and <code>heapLocal_assign</code>, the compositional locality proofs of Unit 26, anything in Modules 2 or 3, <code>hoare_frame</code>, <code>wp</code>, or the two loop rules. Check that claim rather than trusting it — it is the cheapest possible test of whether a development is layered."},

    {t:'note', kind:'info', title:'Project 3 · The two linked-list capstones', h:"On top of Project 2: verify a loop that walks a list and counts its nodes, and a loop that walks a list and frees them. <b>Scope:</b> about 100 lines each, and none of it is new mathematics — <code>lseg</code>, <code>lseg_append</code>, <code>lseg_listRep</code> and the variant rule are all proved. What is missing is loop bookkeeping: the invariants below, the step obligation that moves one node from one side of the cut to the other, and the arithmetic that relates the index to the length of the remaining list. <b>The hard part is not the heap</b> — it is that the invariant has to be a family indexed by the number of nodes left, and each member is an existential over the cursor and over the two pieces of the list."},

    {t:'txt', cap:'The two invariants, written as families indexed by the turns remaining. <code>xs</code> is the list the program was handed and <code>p</code> its head, both fixed; <code>ys</code> is the part already walked, <code>zs</code> the part still to come, and <code>c</code> the address currently in the cursor. <code>cur</code> and <code>n</code> are the two program variables. The first invariant is Unit 33&rsquo;s <code>lseg_append</code> waiting to be used; the second needs only <code>listRep</code>, which is why disposal is the easier of the two.',
     src:"length:   I k  =  ∃ ys zs c.  pure (fun σ => xs = ys ++ zs ∧ zs.length = k\n                                          ∧ σ cur = c ∧ σ n = ys.length)\n                              ∗ lseg ys p c ∗ listRep zs c\n\ndispose:  I k  =  ∃ zs c.     pure (fun σ => zs.length = k ∧ σ cur = c)\n                              ∗ listRep zs c"},

    {t:'p', h:"The step obligation for the first says: one turn reads <code>[c + 1]</code>, moves the node at <code>c</code> from the front of <code>zs</code> to the back of <code>ys</code>, and increments the counter. Moving the node is <code>lseg_append</code> applied to a one-element segment. The stop obligation says that at index zero the cursor is null, so <code>zs</code> is empty by <code>listRep_null</code> and the counter holds the full length. Both halves exist in this course; assembling them is the project."},

    /* ============================================== closing ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The ideas worth keeping'},

    {t:'ul', items:[
      "<b>Ownership is exact.</b> <code>l ↦ v</code> says <i>the heap is this one cell</i>, not <i>this cell is among the ones I have</i>. Unit 07 posed the choice, Unit 13 made it, and Unit 23 showed what it bought: rules whose preconditions are the whole footprint.",
      "<b>Separation implies non-aliasing.</b> <code>l₁ ↦ v₁ ∗ l₂ ↦ v₂</code> <i>proves</i> <code>l₁ ≠ l₂</code>. Side conditions that classical Hoare logic writes on the outside of a rule became theorems on the inside of an assertion.",
      "<b>Small specifications scale.</b> The one-cell write rule of Unit 23 is unchanged in a heap of a million cells. The growing is done once, by a structural rule, rather than in every specification.",
      "<b>The frame rule is a theorem about the semantics — and it is losable.</b> It holds because every command in this language is local. Add an allocator whose specification names the cell it took and the rule becomes false, refuted in four lines that never mention the command.",
      "<b>Recursive predicates fuse shape and ownership.</b> One assertion says <i>these pointers form a list</i> and <i>I own every cell in it</i>, and induction over the list is induction over the ownership.",
      "<b><code>∗</code> and <code>-∗</code> are an adjoint pair.</b> Not two connectives with a lemma between them; one adjunction, from which the algebra follows.",
      "<b>The resource is a parameter.</b> Heaps were one choice of <code>M</code>. The connective, its commutativity and its unit law are properties of the algebra, so a new resource model — permissions, tokens, protocol states — inherits them, and this page proves that rather than promising it."
    ]},

    {t:'dod', h:"You can exhibit a specification that makes the frame rule false, with a satisfiable precondition, and prove it false without writing a semantics for the command. You can point at which of <code>hoare_frame</code>'s three hypotheses fails and say why allocation cannot satisfy it. You can state the three repairs and name a theorem each one costs. You can define <code>∗</code> over an arbitrary partial commutative monoid, prove its commutativity and unit law from the structure fields alone, and say which two heap lemmas each field replaced. You can name two laws that do <i>not</i> follow — associativity and cancellation — and produce a PCM refuting each. You can build a resource algebra of your own and use both theorems on it. And you can give the one-line change to <code>Cmd</code> that lets a program follow a pointer, name the six theorems it obliges you to redo, and say why nothing stated over an arbitrary command is among them."},

    {t:'p', h:"That is the course. It began with a Hoare rule that memory makes false and ends with a frame rule that allocation makes false, and the distance between those two sentences is the whole subject: a logic in which the side conditions are theorems, the specifications are small, and the model is a parameter."},

    {t:'p', h:"Two pages remain and neither is a lecture. <b>§ Tactics</b> is every tactic, keyword and syntactic form in the course, one line each, with the unit that introduced it — and the standing rule that anything not on it does not exist here. <b>§ Reference</b> is the notation table with how to type each symbol, the theorem index, and the five rules of proof discipline. There is no next unit to hand anything to."}

  ]
});
